--[[
	UnifiedCarHandler — REWORKED POWERTRAIN VERSION

	==========================================================================
	PHYSICS ARCHITECTURE
	==========================================================================

	Forward power path (each frame):

	    Throttle pedal
	        v
	    EngineSim            -- combustion torque (from real Nm torque curve),
	        v                   minus friction/pumping/accessory losses and
	                            clutch reaction torque, integrated through
	                            engine rotational inertia -> engine RPM emerges
	    ClutchSim             -- transmits torque based on (engineOmega -
	        v                   transInputOmega) slip, capped at clutch torque
	                            capacity (pedal-scaled)
	    TransmissionSim        -- multiplies torque by gear ratio, applies
	        v                   torque interrupt during a shift
	    (finalDrive, efficiency already folded into TransmissionSim output)
	        v
	    DifferentialSim         -- splits driveshaft torque to the two wheels
	        v                     of a driven axle (OPEN/LOCKED/LIMITED/etc.)
	    WheelSim (per wheel)     -- driveTorque - tireReactionTorque -
	        v                     rollingResistanceTorque - brakeTorque,
	                              integrated through wheel inertia -> wheel
	                              angular velocity emerges
	    TireSim / FrictionSim    -- wheel surface speed vs chassis speed at the
	        v                     contact patch produces slip, slip produces
	                              longitudinal/lateral tire force (Pacejka-like)
	    Roblox chassis (Mass)    -- tire force applied as a native Roblox
	                                Force at the contact attachment; the
	                                existing suspension (TireSim) is untouched

	Feedback path (what makes RPM emerge instead of being scripted):

	    Vehicle speed -> wheel surface speed (via tire slip) -> WheelSim omega
	        -> driveshaft omega (via differential/transmission ratio, used to
	           compute clutch slip) -> clutch reaction torque -> engine load
	        -> engine RPM

	No step in this chain is skipped: acceleration, top speed, engine braking,
	wheelspin and RPM behavior are all *outcomes* of the above relationships,
	not separately scripted values.

	==========================================================================
	UNIT / SCALE NOTES (read this before tuning)
	==========================================================================

	Engine, clutch, transmission, differential and wheel rotation are all
	simulated in real SI units (Nm, kg*m^2, rad/s) with no dependency on studs
	at all -- a wheel's angular velocity in rad/s means the same thing
	regardless of how big the Roblox part is.

	The ONLY place SI has to touch Roblox's own physics is at the tire
	contact patch, where a longitudinal/lateral force has to be written into
	`tire.FrictionForce.Force` / `tire.TireForce.Force` (Roblox VectorForce
	instances acting on the physical chassis). FrictionSim's grip budget
	(FxMax/FyMax) is derived from `mass.AssemblyMass * workspace.Gravity`,
	i.e. it already works natively in whatever units Roblox itself is using
	for mass/force on this rig -- that part is deliberately left as Roblox
	units, unconverted, because that is the actual quantity the Roblox
	physics engine needs to move this actual chassis.

	Units.ForceScale is only used for the *reverse* trip: turning that
	Roblox-unit tire force back into real Newtons so it can be fed back as a
	reaction torque (Nm) on the SI-side WheelSim. This requires one
	assumption, `Units.MassScale`, which is the ratio between this rig's
	Roblox `AssemblyMass` numbers and the real-world kilograms you're
	entering in `stats.massKg`. Roblox's default `workspace.Gravity`
	(196.2 studs/s^2) is not 9.81 m/s^2, and Roblox part mass is not
	calibrated to kg out of the box, so this ratio is a per-experience tuning
	constant, not something that can be derived automatically. It defaults to
	1 (assume AssemblyMass ~= kg numerically); if your rig's real-world
	behavior feels too heavy/light, adjust `Units.MassScale` -- everything
	downstream (wheel reaction torque, rolling resistance, drag) scales
	consistently once you do.

	Everything else (torque curve values, gear ratios, final drive, wheel
	radius, mass, Cd, frontal area) is plain real-world data. Change any of
	it and the simulation responds -- there is no per-car acceleration
	number anywhere in this file.
--]]

